  "title": "What should a creature look like: five body plans, same colony",
  "question": "Which of these reads as an animal you can find and follow? A is what ships today — can you find one?",
  "context_md": "**The question: what should a creature look like?** Five body plans, same\nworld, same seed, same colony, same 600 frames, same crop. Walking, so use the\nscrubber — one of the four creature cards in this queue's history came back\n\"need an animation to tell\".\n\n**A is what ships today.** Everything here is authored data — `body:` in a\nspecies file and a palette in a material file. No engine code changed.\n\nThe scene is deliberately the *easiest* picture a small dark body will ever be\nin: bare grey rock against a lit sky. The colony picture that started this was a\nvegetated wetland, which is harder.\n\n**What the numbers under each pane mean.** `ink` is how much luminance the\nbodies put on screen that the ground would not have put there anyway — measured\nby rendering the same world twice, with the animals and without. `decoys` is the\none that explains the picture: how many *other* places in this frame are at\nleast as different from their surroundings as the animal is. A pixel-art world's\ntexture is 1–2 cell luminance noise, so a 2-cell animal is competing with every\nrock edge and every leaf.\n\nAt the contrast a dark body actually achieves here, holding the threshold fixed\nand moving only the body's size:\n\n| body size | decoys in one frame |\n|---|---|\n| 1 cell | 337 |\n| **2 cells (ships today)** | **126** |\n| 4 cells | 57 |\n| 9 cells | 13 |\n| 16 cells | 0 |\n\n**E is the palette arm and it is the same body as C**, bit-identical run, only\nthe colours differ — so the pair prices hue/value on its own. It costs 9 cells\nand puts *less* on screen than the 2-cell ant does.\n",
